  "certificateReading": {
    "smartcityos.io": "chainVerified=true, subjectCN=smartcityos.io, issuer=Google Trust Services 'WE1', SAN=DNS:smartcityos.io, validTo 2026-12-16T20:30:00Z. A real, publicly-trusted certificate.",
    "www.smartcityos.io": "chainVerified=true, subjectCN=www.smartcityos.io, issuer=Google Trust Services 'WE1', SAN=DNS:www.smartcityos.io, validTo 2026-12-16T20:31:55Z.",
    "discrepancyWorthCarrying": "DigitalOcean's apps API still reports the apex with certificate_expires_at = 1970-01-01 (its own ensure-certificate step ended in DomainOwnershipVerificationErr) while www reports 2026-12-16. Both hostnames nevertheless serve a valid certificate. The API field tracks DigitalOcean's OWN issuance attempt; the certificate the edge actually presents for a Cloudflare-fronted custom hostname is obtained through Cloudflare's custom-hostname flow. Consequence: certificate_expires_at is not a valid way to judge whether a customer gets a good certificate, and a successor should check the chain from outside instead. The likely reading is that the apex certificate is edge-issued; this lane measured the observable (a valid chain) and did not have a vantage point inside DigitalOcean to confirm the issuer of record.",
    "renewalRisk": "Both certificates expire 2026-12-16. The apex is the one to watch: whatever renews it is not the step DigitalOcean reports as failed. Re-check the apex chain well before that date rather than assuming renewal."
  },
